## Why Compos

Most programs make you leave your work to find the next piece of information.
You switch to a browser, then to a chat window, then to a terminal, and you
rebuild the task in your head each time.

Compos does it differently. The information comes to you, in another window,
without your leaving what you are doing.

Emacs, the editor Compos is modelled on, was built this way long before agents
existed. A programmer could keep code in one window and documentation in
another, and read the documentation without leaving the code. Mail, help,
shells, directories and source all lived in the same program, and the same
movement and editing commands worked in all of them.

Compos keeps that idea and adds a graphical display, work that runs at the
same time, saved state, and agents. An agent is a model that works on the same
buffers you do. A web page, a file, a chat, a mail thread, a search result and
a running process are all buffers in one system.

Every one of them has a name, named commands, a place on the screen, and state
that you and an agent can both change.

Every window, list and page uses the same movement and editing keys. So the
next sections teach those keys, on the text in front of you. When they are
familiar, the sections on Windows, Lists and peeks, Groups and Agents show the
idea above in action.

The tutorial is a buffer too. You are learning Compos from inside Compos.

## Key notation

Some commands use more than one key. A space between keys means "then", not
"at the same time".

For example, C-x C-f means:

  1. Hold CONTROL and press x, then release both keys.
  2. Hold CONTROL and press f, then release both keys.

C-x is called a prefix key. It tells Compos to wait for the next key. The next
key chooses the command.

Some keys are written by name:

  RET    Return
  SPC    Space
  DEL    Backspace

Angle brackets name a key on the keyboard: <down>, <up>, <left> and <right>
are the arrow keys. s- means hold the Super key, which is Command on macOS.

If a prefix, a prompt or a search waits for input you do not want to give,
type C-g. A lone Escape also quits, but C-g is the habit to keep.

>> Hold CONTROL and press x, then release both keys.
   Compos is now waiting for the next key.
   Type C-g to cancel.

You started a prefix command and cancelled it.

## Named commands

Every action in Compos is a named command. A key binding, a menu, a Scheme
program, an agent, a test, or an outside client can all run the same command.
The key is only one way to ask for the action.

M-x asks for a command by name. The list gets shorter as you type, and it
shows each command's key beside the name. So using names teaches you the keys.

>> Type M-x, then type recenter, then choose recenter-top-bottom and press
   RET. The line at point moves on the screen, but point stays on the same
   text.

You never need to know a command before you use it. Type the words you would
use to describe what you want.

>> Type M-x, then type tutorial, and notice help-with-tutorial with C-h t
   beside it. Type C-g to leave the command picker.

Agents use this same list. An agent does not guess which rectangle is a
button. It finds and runs the named commands you use.

## Moving by screens

A buffer often holds more text than one window can show. The window shows one
screenful; the rest continues above and below. Scrolling moves the view over
the text. It never changes the text.

>> Type C-v once to move forward almost one screen.
   Then type M-v once to move backward and return here.

  C-v    Move forward almost one screen
  M-v    Move backward almost one screen
  C-l    Put the line at point in the middle of the screen (lowercase L)

>> Type C-l and watch this line move to the middle.
   The text does not change.

When you scroll a whole screen, the lines at the edge stay, so you can keep
reading without losing your place.

The modeline at the bottom of the window shows the buffer name, the size and
point, and whether you are near the top, middle or bottom of the buffer.

## Moving point

  C-f    Move forward one character
  C-b    Move backward one character
  C-n    Move to the next line
  C-p    Move to the previous line
  C-a    Move to the start of the line
  C-e    Move to the end of the line
  M-f    Move forward one word
  M-b    Move backward one word
  M-<    Move to the start of the buffer
  M->    Move to the end of the buffer

The letters are easy to remember: P for previous, N for next, B for backward
and F for forward. You will use these commands all the time.

>> Move through this paragraph with C-f, C-b, C-n and C-p only.
   Then use M-f and M-b to travel by words.
   Finish with C-a and C-e.

The command names are literal: forward-char, backward-char, next-line,
previous-line, forward-word. A key is a short name for a command, not a
second interface you must learn.

>> Type M-> to visit the end of the tutorial and read the last line.
   Type M-< to come back.

## Windows

Compos can show more than one window at a time. A window shows a buffer.
Splitting a window does not copy the buffer, and closing a window does not
kill the buffer.

  C-x 2    Split the window below
  C-x 3    Split the window to the right
  C-x o    Move to the other window
  C-x b    Show another buffer in this window
  C-x 0    Close the selected window
  C-x 1    Close every other window

>> Type C-x 2 to split the screen into two windows.
   Compos may show another buffer in the new window.
   Type C-x o, then C-x b, choose TUTORIAL and press RET.
   Both windows now show TUTORIAL.
   Move point in the second window.
   Each window remembers its own point, even though both show the same text.

One small command shows the whole idea:

  C-M-v     Scroll the other window while you stay in this one
  M-<down>  The same
  M-<up>    Scroll the other window backward

>> Stay in this window and type C-M-v a few times.
   Watch the other view move while your point and your focus stay here.
   Then type M-<up> once.

You did not leave your work to read the reference. You brought the reference
beside the work and controlled it. This is the promise from the start of the
tutorial: the context comes to you. The same command can scroll a help page, a
peek (a preview beside the work, covered later), the messages buffer or any
other useful neighbour.

>> Type C-x 1 to return to one window.

## Editing and undo

Typing inserts text at point.

  DEL    Delete the character before point
  C-d    Delete the character after point
  M-d    Kill the next word
  C-k    Kill from point to the end of the line

Put point after the >> on the line below and type your name:

>>

To undo the last change, type C-/. C-x u does the same. Type C-/ again to undo
one change more. After any command other than undo, the next undo goes forward
again. So undo after a break is redo.

>> Type three words on the blank line below.
   Delete a few characters.
   Type C-/ until the line is empty again.


Every command is one undo step. Typing joins into groups, and commands such as
newline and kill make useful boundaries. Edit freely; the editor keeps the
history.

## Prefix arguments

Most Compos commands accept a count. To give one, type C-u and then the digits,
before you type the command. With a META key there is a shorter way: hold META
and type the digits.

>> Type C-u 5 C-n. Point moves down five lines.
   Type M-3 C-p to move back three lines.

A command uses the count in different ways. Movement, deletion, killing,
scrolling and undo repeat. A command that takes no count may read C-u as a
request for another version of itself.

>> Type C-u 5 C-v to scroll five lines.
   Then type C-u 5 M-v to scroll back.

You do not need prefix arguments often. The point is that commands compose: a
small set of movements and edits expresses large actions.

## Killing and yanking

To set the mark, type C-SPC. If macOS uses C-SPC to switch input sources, type
M-SPC instead. Move point after you set the mark, and the text between the mark
and point becomes the active region.

  C-w        Kill the region
  M-w        Copy the region and leave it in place
  C-y        Yank the most recent killed text
  M-y        Replace that yank with an older kill
  C-x C-x    Exchange point and mark

>> Put point at the start of this sentence, type C-SPC, move to the end with
   C-e, and type C-w.
   Then type C-y to bring the sentence back.

Killing is more useful than deleting, because killed text goes into a shared
kill ring. Do several kills in a row and they join into one entry. So C-k C-k
C-k removes several lines, and one C-y restores them together.

The mark also remembers places. C-u C-SPC returns to earlier marks in this
buffer. C-x C-SPC walks the global mark ring across buffers.

>> Set the mark here, move somewhere else in the tutorial, then type C-u
   C-SPC to return.
   Type C-g if you wanted the jump only, without an active region.

## Searching and replacing

To search forward, type C-s. To search backward, type C-r. Each character you
type makes the match more specific at once. Search is a movement command; it
does not make you fill a form before you see a result.

>> Type C-s, then type "window", and watch point move as the match becomes
   specific.
   Type C-s again for the next match, or C-g to stop.

While you search, RET accepts the match. C-g quits and returns to where the
search started, where that makes sense.

M-% runs query-replace. It asks for the old text and the new text, then visits
each match, so you choose which ones to change.

>> Put point at the start of the next line.
   Type M-%, replace "colour" with "color", and answer y for the match.
   Type C-/ afterward if you want the original text back.

This practice line uses the word colour once.

## Files and buffers

Compos checkpoints buffers, including unsaved ones, so their text can survive
a restart. Put text in a file when it should also exist outside Compos and have
a path that other programs can open.

A file is data on disk. A buffer is a named place where the editor holds text.
A file buffer visits the file it came from: editing the buffer changes the
buffer, and C-x C-s writes the buffer back to the file.

  C-x C-f    Find or create a file
  C-x C-s    Save the current file buffer
  C-x b      Switch buffers
  C-x k      Kill a buffer

This TUTORIAL buffer does not visit the master file. So your edits cannot
change the installed tutorial.

>> Type C-x C-f and enter /tmp/compos-practice.txt.
   Type a line and save it with C-x C-s.
   Then type C-x b, choose TUTORIAL and press RET to return.

Almost everything you see in Compos lives in a buffer. Files are one kind.
Help pages, chats, directory listings, web pages, mail, process output, search
results and dashboards are buffers too.

Because of that, they share the same window commands, links, search, modes,
saved state, groups and agent context.

