  C-h b    List the bindings active here
  C-h B    Open the keymap browser, which you can edit
  C-h a    Search commands and functions by words
  M-?      Explain the current context, or the name at point
  C-h t    Open or resume this tutorial

>> Type C-h k C-M-v and read about scroll-other-window.
   Type q to dismiss the help page.

>> Type C-h m in TUTORIAL.
   Notice the mode, the maps in force, and the commands they add.
   Type q when you finish.

M-x apropos searches everything that is defined. A public definition carries a
domain, its effects, a signature and documentation, so people and agents can
both find it.

>> Type M-x apropos, search for "other window", and read the results.
   Type q to return to the tutorial.

## Modes and keymaps

A major mode gives a buffer its main interpretation: text, Scheme, chat,
browse, help, a list. A minor mode adds separate behaviour, such as previews
or editing conventions.

Behaviour is not fixed to a set of application types. It composes from named
maps, modes, hooks, buffer-local values and commands.

When you type a key, Compos looks for it in this order: temporary maps, the
thing at point, minor modes, the buffer and its major mode, read-only
behaviour, then global bindings. Context can change a familiar key without
throwing away the common interface.

>> Type C-h B.
   The keymap browser shows every binding in force here, which map supplied
   it, and whether another binding shadows it.
   Type / to filter for "tutorial" or "scroll". Type q to leave.

So a list, a source file, a chat and a browser page can differ where they
should, and stay consistent where they can.

## Lists and peeks

Compos applications share one list interface:

  n          Move to the next row
  p          Move to the previous row
  /          Narrow the rows as you type
  Backslash  Remove the newest filter
  SPC        Mark items, when the list supports selection
  g          Refresh, when the list supports refreshing

A list mode adds its own actions, such as RET to visit a row and q to quit.
A list mode can also give a shared key a different action. C-n and C-p
always move by rows.

>> Type M-x ibuffer.
   This list manages buffers. Move through the rows with C-n and C-p. In
   ibuffer, p turns the row preview on and off. Type / and part of a
   buffer name, press RET to accept the filter, then press Backslash to widen
   the list again.

A peek shows you something without switching context. A list such as dired can
show the row at point in another window, while your selection stays in the
list. The next peek replaces the previous one, and q dismisses it. M-<down>
and M-<up> scroll the peek without taking your focus.

This is scroll-other-window made into a pattern: look at nearby context
without losing the work that led you there.

>> Type C-x b TUTORIAL RET when you are ready to return.

## Browsing the web

M-x browse opens a URL, or a search, as readable Markdown in a browse buffer.
The page on the web stays the source of truth, and preview mode gives it a
graphical reading surface.

>> Type M-x browse and enter https://example.com.
   When the page appears, try TAB, n and p to move among links.
   Type q to return to the tutorial.

In browse mode:

  RET          Follow a link in the current browse tab
  s-RET        Open a separate browse tab (Command-RET on macOS)
  M-RET        Peek the link in another window
  l            Go back (Meta-Left does the same)
  Meta-Right   Go forward
  R            Toggle calm and full readings
  o            Open the page in the outside browser
  w            Copy the link at point, or the page URL
  H            Open the reading history
  C-c C-v      Cycle the view: monospace, serif, then Markdown source

The web is not reduced to a screenshot, and an agent does not have to guess at
DOM selectors. The readable page has a buffer, named commands, links, history,
point and a mode, like everything else here.

## Groups

A group remembers a task: its buffers, its companion chat, and the window
arrangement you left behind. Switching to a group restores the context, not
one document.

  M-x group-new    Create and enter a group
  C-x g            Switch groups
  C-x C-g C-g      Return to the previous group
  C-x C-g b        List the buffers in the current group
  C-x C-g s        Tile the group

>> Type M-x group-new and call the group tutorial-practice.
   With nothing marked, the new group starts empty and opens its scratch
   buffer.
   Type C-x g to look at the group switcher, then C-g if you do not want
   to switch.
   Type C-x b, choose TUTORIAL and press RET to return here.

A task should not be a pile of tabs that you rebuild from memory. Its sources,
notes, pages, process output and conversation should come back together.

A buffer can belong to several useful contexts without being trapped in one
application.

## Agents

Before you try this section, type M-x setup-ai-guide. Choose a connector,
complete its login or key setup, and test one reply in a chat. Return here
with C-h t. A connector in a list may still need authentication.

In most programs, a chat sidebar and an application share a screen but stay
separate systems. The assistant sees summaries and a few narrow actions, while
the application keeps the real state somewhere else.

Compos begins with one environment. The application and the conversation are
buffers in the same group. You and the agent act on the same named commands
and the same state.

The commands in this section are optional. Your provider may charge for
requests. A chat is the conversation buffer. A model writes responses. An
agent uses that model with tools to work in Compos.

  M-x chat-new             Start a new chat buffer
  C-c b                    Choose the chat's connector, model, effort, tools
                           and permissions
  M-x agent-switch         Move a running chat to another connector and model
  C-c w                    Switch between a work buffer and its chat
  M-x chat-companion-ask   Ask the companion a question without leaving the
                           work buffer
  M-|                      Send the active region through an LLM

A connector is how Compos reaches an agent or model. The connector picker
shows the supported connectors below. A connector works when its program and
credentials are available, or when its browser model can run. Test a reply
before you rely on the connector.

  codex-app-server  Codex through codex app-server. It uses the Codex CLI's
                    existing ChatGPT login; it needs no API key.
  claude-code       Claude Code through ACP. It needs a configured adapter and
                    a Claude login.
  opencode          OpenCode through ACP. It uses the providers configured in
                    OpenCode.
  deepseek          DeepSeek Harness through ACP. It needs an installed and
                    configured DSH profile.
  api               Direct model APIs for providers whose keys you configured
                    in Compos.
  gemini-nano       The local browser model, when the browser provides it.

ACP is the Agent Client Protocol. It lets Compos start an external coding
agent as a process and exchange structured messages, tools and permissions
with it. Codex now uses its app-server protocol instead. The older codex-acp
connector is deprecated and is not shown in the picker.

>> Type M-x chat-new. In the new chat, type C-c b. The left column lists
   saved presets, and the right column shows this chat's setup.
   Press <right> to move to the setup. Press b and choose codex-app-server.
   Press m and choose a model, or choose default for the connector default.
   Press ESC to give the setup to the chat.
   Type a message and press RET to send it.

If you chose Codex, sign in with codex login in a terminal first. The AI
starter guide explains how to check the login when a chat returns 401.

>> From TUTORIAL, type M-x chat-companion-ask and ask: "What is the main idea
   of the section named Windows?"
   The companion reads the live tutorial buffer, so you do not paste it into
   another program.

>> Open the practice file from the Files and buffers section with C-x C-f
   /tmp/compos-practice.txt.
   Type M-x chat-companion-ask there and ask: "Summarize this file."
   The companion reads the file buffer. You did not copy any of it into the
