Agents also write extensions. Ask for one and watch it appear as a command:

>> Type M-x chat-companion-ask and ask: "Define a Scheme command named
   tutorial-agent-hello that prints a greeting."
   Then type M-x tutorial-agent-hello.

The agent wrote the definition with the same Scheme evaluator the editor
itself runs. The context is concrete: it is there in the buffer.

## Persistence

Compos runs as a daemon. It does not lose its state like a web page that you
close.

Durable buffers, frame window trees, points, chats, groups and selected local
state survive a restart. Compos puts back the saved theme, and browser clients
reconnect to their frames.

Buffer checkpoints keep file paths and live contents, including unsaved edits.
Buffers that visit no file are checkpointed too. A mode rebuilds its live
behaviour around the restored state.

Your work is not a form submission, and your chat is not an archive behind a
settings page. When you leave and return, the world you built is still there.

The tutorial follows the same rule. Normal checkpoints preserve a live
TUTORIAL buffer without asking. When its point or text has changed, C-x k asks
whether to keep this copy and position for C-h t.

One thing does not come back this way: a definition you add at runtime. The
next section explains why.

## The editor is malleable

Compos is not only a fixed editor. It is a live Scheme environment. Loading a
file is evaluation, and a symbol is a stable name whose definition can change
while the system runs. The evaluator is part of the product.

  M-:        Evaluate one Scheme expression
  C-x C-e    Evaluate the expression before point, in a Scheme buffer

>> Type M-:, enter the expression below, and press RET.

   (message "Hello from the live Scheme environment")

The message appears at once. No build, restart, bundler or plugin host was
needed.

You can make a command the same way.

>> Type M-:, enter the following expression as one line, and press RET.

   (define-command "tutorial-hello" "Say hello from the tutorial" (lambda () (message "The running editor has a new command")))

>> Now type M-x tutorial-hello.

The definition lasts until you restart, unless you put it in
~/.compos/init.scm or in a project's compos.scm. A buffer holds data, and the
daemon brings data back from its checkpoints. A definition is part of the
running program, and the program starts again from your init files. So try a
change live first, then keep it when it proves useful. An agent can make the
same small extension for you.

In Compos, a missing workflow can begin as a few lines of Scheme and grow only
as far as its usefulness demands.

## Scheme and the BEAM

One rule explains the whole system:

Elixir supplies mechanism. Scheme decides policy.

Scheme owns what you experience as the editor: commands, keymaps, modes,
hooks, completion, display rules, themes, chats, mail, browser policy,
permission prompts and applications.

Elixir and OTP own the mechanisms that move bytes, cross operating-system
boundaries, or need reliable concurrent lifetimes: ropes, parsers, sockets,
PTYs, schedulers, model transport, buffers as processes, and supervision.

So Compos needs no large plugin lifecycle framework of the JavaScript kind.
Lisp already supplies symbols, late binding, live evaluation, dynamic context
and inspectable state. Ordinary definitions and registrations stay ordinary.
External resources such as processes, sockets and timers get explicit owners
through OTP.

The boundary keeps the system open without making the runtime fragile. Scheme
can redefine what a key means while the editor runs. OTP can stop a slow model
stream, or a failing process, from freezing the interface.

The browser is a renderer and a client, not the editor. The headless daemon is
the system of record. The browser UI, the desktop shell and RPC clients address
the same core, and another client, such as a TUI, can use it too.

## Where to go next

You have practised the main ideas:

  - Information comes to you, through buffers, windows, peeks, browser tabs
    and groups.
  - Named commands give people and agents one place to act.
  - Modes and keymaps specialize a common interface without splitting it.
  - A conversation lives beside the state it discusses.
  - The live Scheme environment lets the editor grow around your work.
  - OTP supplies durable, concurrent mechanisms below that policy.

You do not need to remember every key. Keep using M-x to find commands, C-h to
look at the current world, and C-g to leave anything unfinished.

To leave the tutorial, type C-x k. If you moved or edited it, Compos asks
whether to save this copy and your position. Type C-h t whenever you want to
come back. If you decline a saved copy, the next C-h t loads the installed
master.

For setup and the full documentation, read README.md, docs/README.md and
docs/LEARN COMPOS.md in the Compos source checkout.

The best next step is real work. Find a file with C-x C-f, bring one useful
piece of context beside it, and ask the companion to help you make the
environment fit the task.

This tutorial aims to be clear to a new user. If some part is not clear, do not
sit and blame yourself. Report it, and the text will improve.
